Wave-washed deposits
Soil properties for one of the hydrogeological environments described in Lundmark, A., 
2008. Monitoring transport and fate of de-icing salt in the roadside environment - Modelling 
and field measurements. Doctoral thesis in Land and Water Resources Engineering, 
TRITA-LWR PHD 1038. KTH Architecture and the Built Environment.

Conceptual description:
These deposits are situated below the highest marine shoreline and are composed of 
wave-washed secondary deposits, which are mainly sandy, but can also consist of gravel.
They occur laterally on eskers, often underlain by clay soils, or along hillslopes where the 
till has been wave-washed. The thickness is usually 1-2 m. The groundwater level is often 
close to the ground surface as the deposits are underlain by clay that prevents further 
percolation of the water. The areas can be overgrown by coniferous forest, assumed 
dominated by pine, or used as arable land.

Soil profile:
Sand 0-2 m depth
Clay 2-7 m depth
Sand and gravel 7-10 m depth
Bedrock >10 m depth

Brooks and Corey parameters for sand, till and clay represent effective parameters
reported in Lundmark & Jansson: Generic soil descriptions for modelling water and chloride
dynamics in the unsaturated zone based on Swedish soils. Submitted December 2007 to
Geoderma. 

2008-03-17 Annika Lundmark
